{"name":"Corallite","hex":"#f0dfcd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"corallite","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The hard outer cup of a coral polyp"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/corallite"}],"entries":[{"title":"Corallite","blocks":[{"text":"A corallite is the skeletal cup, formed by an individual stony coral polyp, in which the polyp sits and into which it can retract. The cup is composed of aragonite, a crystalline form of calcium carbonate, and is secreted by the polyp. Corallites vary in size, but in most colonial corals they are less than 3 mm (0.12 in) in diameter. The inner surface of the corallite is known as the calyx. The vertical blades inside the calyx are known as septa and in some species, these ridges continue outside the corallite wall as costae. Where there is no corallite wall, the blades are known as septocostae. The septa, costae and septocostae may have ornamentation in the form of teeth and may be thick, thin or variable in size. Sometimes there are paliform lobes, in the form of rods or blades, rising from the inner margins of the septa. These may form a neat circle called the paliform crown. The septa do not usually unite in the centre of the corallite, instead they form a columella, a tangled mass of intertwined septa, or a dome-shaped or pillar-like projection. In the living coral, the lower part of the polyp is in intimate contact with the corallite, and has radial mesenteries between the septa which increase the surface area of the body cavity and aid digestion. The septa, palliform lobes and costae can often be seen through the coenosarc, the layer of living tissue that covers the coenosteum, the part of the skeleton between the corallites."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/49/Coral_polyp.jpg/330px-Coral_polyp.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corallite"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Astrangia_danae.jpeg/330px-Astrangia_danae.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Astrangia_danae.jpeg/330px-Astrangia_danae.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Up : zoom on the skeletal cup of an Astrangia coral; Down : view of the skeleton of the whole colony, showing all the corallites. ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAstrangia_danae.jpeg"}],"sounds":{},"pictures":[{"title":"Werner's nomenclature of colours greys","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Werner%27s_nomenclature_of_colours_greys.jpg/500px-Werner%27s_nomenclature_of_colours_greys.jpg","ratio":0.794047619047619,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Werner%27s_nomenclature_of_colours_greys.jpg","share":0.724375},{"title":"On the structure and affinities of the genus Monticulipora and its sub-genera, with critical descriptions of illustrative species (1881) (14596375527)","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/On_the_structure_and_affinities_of_the_genus_Monticulipora_and_its_sub-genera%2C_with_critical_descriptions_of_illustrative_species_%281881%29_%2814596375527%29.jpg/500px-On_the_structure_and_affinities_of_the_genus_Monticulipora_and_its_sub-genera%2C_with_critical_descriptions_of_illustrative_species_%281881%29_%2814596375527%29.jpg","ratio":0.5781155015197569,"link":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:On_the_structure_and_affinities_of_the_genus_Monticulipora_and_its_sub-genera,_with_critical_descriptions_of_illustrative_species_(1881)_(14596375527).jpg","share":0.289375},{"title":"Hexagonaria sp. (fossil coral) with petroleum (Dundee Limestone, Middle Devonian; quarry northwest of Paulding, Ohio, USA) 4 (48319406952)","image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Hexagonaria_sp._%28fos